  "topic": "Move Semantics and Rvalue References",
  "explanation": "In modern C++, move semantics enable efficient transfer of resources from temporary objects (rvalues) to new objects, reducing unnecessary copying and improving performance. Rvalue references (denoted by &&) are a key feature that allow functions and constructors to distinguish between lvalues and rvalues, facilitating move semantics. This is especially important in resource-managing classes, such as containers and smart pointers, to optimize resource transfer without expensive copying.",
  "syntax": "Class with move constructor:\n\nclass ClassName {\npublic:\n    // Move constructor\n    ClassName(ClassName&& other) noexcept;\n    // Move assignment operator\n    ClassName& operator=(ClassName&& other) noexcept;\n};\n\nFunction returning rvalue reference:\n\nType&& functionName();",
  "code_example": [
    "#include <iostream>",
    "#include <vector>",
    "",
    "class Buffer {",
    "public:",
    "    std::vector<int> data_;",
    "    // Constructor",
    "    Buffer() = default;",
    "    // Move constructor",
    "    Buffer(Buffer&& other) noexcept : data_(std::move(other.data_)) {",
    "        std::cout << \"Move constructor called\" << std::endl;",
    "    }",
    "    // Move assignment",
    "    Buffer& operator=(Buffer&& other) noexcept {",
    "        if (this != &other) {",
    "            data_ = std::move(other.data_);",
    "            std::cout << \"Move assignment called\" << std::endl;",
    "        }",
    "        return *this;",
    "    }",
    "};",
    "",
    "Buffer createBuffer() {",
    "    Buffer buf;",
    "    buf.data_.push_back(42);",
    "    return buf; // Rvalue returned here",
    "}",
    "",
    "int main() {",
    "    Buffer b1 = createBuffer(); // Move constructor invoked",
    "    Buffer b2;",
    "    b2 = createBuffer(); // Move assignment invoked",
    "    return 0;",
    "}"
